In this exercise, we learn how to visualise data distribution using two methods: ridgeline plot and raincloud plot.
A ridgeline plot is useful when we want to compare the distribution of one numeric variable across many groups. A raincloud plot is useful because it combines density shape, boxplot summary, and individual data points in one chart.
By the end of this exercise, we should be able to:

create ridgeline plots using ggridges;
create raincloud plots using ggdist;
compare distribution shapes across different groups;
explain what the chart tells us.

A ternary plot is used to visualise data made up of three parts. The three components are placed inside a triangle, and each point represents how the total is divided among the three components.
This type of chart is useful when the main question is not about absolute size, but about composition. For example, a population area can be described by the proportion of young residents, economically active residents and old residents.
In this hands-on exercise, ternary plots are used to study the population structure of Singapore. The focus is on comparing the balance between three age groups across planning areas and subzones.
By the end of this exercise, we should be able to:

prepare population data for ternary plotting;
derive young, active and old population measures;
create a static ternary plot using ggtern;
improve the ternary plot with labels, colour and theme settings;
create an interactive ternary plot using plotly;
interpret population structure from a compositional point of view.

Time-oriented data can be viewed from several angles. Some tasks focus on daily intensity, some focus on repeated seasonal patterns, and some focus on change between two points in time.
In this hands-on exercise, three visual techniques are used:

calendar heatmap for event intensity by date;
cycle plot for seasonal arrivals by month;
slopegraph for comparing rice yield change between two years.

By the end of this exercise, we should be able to:

prepare timestamp fields for time-based visualisation;
build calendar heatmaps with ggplot2;
reshape monthly arrival data for cycle plots;
compare start and end values using slopegraphs;
interpret time patterns without relying only on a standard line chart.

Parallel coordinates plot is a useful visual analytics method for exploring multivariate numerical data. Instead of looking at one variable at a time, this method places several numerical variables on parallel vertical axes and connects each observation across the axes.
This is especially useful when the analyst wants to compare many variables together, detect possible clusters, identify unusual observations, and understand how different indicators move across countries or groups.
In this hands-on exercise, the World Happiness 2018 data will be used to demonstrate both static and interactive parallel coordinates plots.
The main learning points are:

how to create static parallel coordinates plots using ggparcoord() from the GGally package;
how to scale variables so that different numerical ranges can be compared fairly;
how to use grouping, transparency, boxplots, facets, and axis-label adjustment;
how to create interactive parallel coordinates plots using the parallelPlot package.

