8 Summary
Treemap is suitable when the data has a clear hierarchy. In this exercise, the hierarchy is based on planning region, planning area, and project name.
The key technical points are:

group_by() and summarise() convert transaction-level data into aggregated data;
index defines the hierarchy in the treemap package;
vSize controls rectangle size;
vColor controls rectangle colour;
type = "value" maps colour intensity according to a numerical variable;
type = "manual" gives more direct control over the colour palette;
palette controls the colour scheme;
algorithm controls the treemap layout;
sortID controls rectangle ordering;
treemapify provides a cleaner ggplot2-style approach;
limiting the number of project labels is necessary for readability.

Correlation analysis is used to examine how two numerical variables move together. A correlation coefficient ranges from -1 to 1. A value close to 1 means a strong positive linear relationship, a value close to -1 means a strong negative linear relationship, and a value close to 0 means there is no clear linear relationship.
When a dataset contains many numerical variables, checking every pair one by one is inefficient. A correlation matrix helps us compare all pairwise relationships in one structured view. A corrgram then turns this matrix into a visual display, making strong, weak, positive and negative relationships easier to detect.
In this hands-on exercise, we use the wine quality dataset to explore different methods for visualising correlation:

scatterplot matrix using pairs();
correlation matrix using ggstatsplot;
separate correlation matrices for red and white wine;
corrgram using corrplot;
mixed corrgram with both shapes and numbers;
significance testing for correlation pairs;
reordered corrgram to reveal hidden structure.

The main packages used in this exercise are:

tidyverse for data import and wrangling;
corrplot for correlation matrix visualisation;
ggstatsplot for statistical correlation plots;
knitr for displaying tables.


pacman::p_load(corrplot, ggstatsplot, tidyverse, knitr)

In this hands-on exercise, we focus on how uncertainty can be shown visually. A chart often gives a single value, such as a mean score, but that value is only an estimate. To make the analysis more complete, we also need to show how uncertain the estimate is.
This exercise uses error bars, confidence intervals, interactive plots, ggdist interval plots and hypothetical outcome plots. The main example uses Maths scores from the examination dataset.
By the end of this exercise, we should be able to:

calculate summary statistics for point estimates;
visualise standard error bars using ggplot2;
visualise confidence intervals using ggplot2;
create interactive uncertainty plots using plotly, DT and crosstalk;
use ggdist to create interval-based uncertainty graphics;
understand the basic idea of hypothetical outcome plots.

A heatmap is used to visualise values in a table through colour. It is especially useful when a dataset contains many observations and several numerical variables. Instead of reading values one by one, a heatmap allows us to detect high-value and low-value patterns quickly.
In this hands-on exercise, we use the World Happiness 2018 dataset to create static and interactive heatmaps. The main analytical goal is to compare countries across different happiness-related indicators, including happiness score, GDP per capita, social support, healthy life expectancy, freedom, generosity and perceptions of corruption.
By the end of this exercise, we should be able to:

import and prepare the World Happiness 2018 dataset;
select suitable numerical variables for heatmap analysis;
transform a data frame into a matrix;
create static heatmaps using base R;
create interactive heatmaps using heatmaply;
compare scaling, normalising and percentising methods;
apply clustering and seriation methods;
improve the heatmap with a customised colour palette.

Text data is unstructured, so it needs to be reshaped before it can be counted, compared and visualised. The tidy text approach converts documents into one-token-per-row data, making it possible to use familiar dplyr and ggplot2 workflows.
In this hands-on exercise, the 20 Newsgroups sample is used to practise text import, cleaning, tokenisation, word frequency analysis, TF-IDF, word correlation and bigram network visualisation.
By the end of this exercise, we should be able to:

import multiple text files from multiple folders;
clean email-style message text;
tokenise text into individual words and bigrams;
remove stop words and noisy tokens;
compute term frequency and TF-IDF;
visualise word co-occurrence and bigram relationships as networks.

Point maps are used when each observation has a coordinate pair. Unlike choropleth maps, the unit of analysis is a location rather than an area. Symbol size, colour and shape can then be used to encode additional attributes.
In this hands-on exercise, Singapore Pools outlet data is converted from an aspatial table into an sf point layer. The points are mapped over Singapore subzones and then redesigned as proportional symbol maps.
By the end of this exercise, we should be able to:

import point data with projected coordinates;
convert a data frame into an sf point object;
draw point symbol maps;
draw proportional symbol maps;
compare point patterns by outlet type;
use a base polygon layer to provide geographic context.

